<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. van Beethoven asked for a same-day visit because his left ear became muffled two days ago, after a shower, and has stayed blocked. The left hearing aid whistles when he puts it in, and the ringing in that ear is louder. He was frightened that his hearing had suddenly dropped, because he was told at his consultation to come in the same week for any sudden loss. He has no ear pain, discharge, fever, dizziness, or facial weakness, and the right ear is unchanged. He has not used cotton swabs. He has not been near loud sound since the last rehearsal, where he wore his earplugs. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, after a shower</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Left ear</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days, constant</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Blocked, muffled feeling; louder tinnitus on the left</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Cannot use the left hearing aid</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Constant since onset</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Soft wax seen in the left canal at the preventive visit four weeks ago; daily hearing aid use</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>No change with swallowing or yawning</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No pain, discharge, fever, dizziness, or facial weakness; right ear unchanged</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

