  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Impacted cerumen, left ear (H61.22)</content>. A sudden muffling in one ear is first a question of whether the loss is conductive or sensorineural. Here the canal was blocked and the tuning forks showed a conductive pattern on the left, so this was not a sudden sensorineural hearing loss. After irrigation the canal and eardrum are normal, he says the left ear sounds as it did last week, and the tuning-fork findings are back to baseline. An audiogram is not needed today. [S]<list><item>Left ear irrigated with body-temperature water after checking that the eardrum had never been perforated and he had no ear surgery.</item><item>No cotton swabs or ear candles; a few drops of a softening agent in each ear once a week, if wax builds up again.</item><item>Ear canals checked at each visit because he wears hearing aids.</item><item>Come in the same day, or go to urgent care, if hearing drops in either ear and the ear is not blocked: a sudden sensorineural loss should be treated within days.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Sensorineural hearing loss with hearing aids (H90.3)</content>. Left hearing aid cleaned and checked; whistling stopped once the canal was clear. [S]<list><item>Audiology to replace the left dome and check the wax guard this week.</item><item>Repeat audiogram at the six-month visit, as planned.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Adjustment disorder with depressed mood (F43.21)</content>. Frightened by the change but reassured once his hearing returned. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm. [S]<list><item>Continue therapy.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Clinical Practice Guideline: Sudden Hearing Loss (Update) (Chandrasekhar SS, et al. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019;161(1_suppl):S1-S45) (2019); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Clinical Practice Guideline (Update): Earwax (Cerumen Impaction) (Schwartz SR, et al. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017;156(1_suppl):S1-S29) (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-month review in four weeks, as scheduled. Same-day visit for any sudden drop in hearing. Next scheduled visit 1801-10-19: six-month review with repeat audiogram. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
